## Maps in Go

Maps are key-value data structures (hash tables/dictionaries).

### Creating Maps

```go
// make function (recommended)
ages := make(map[string]int)

// Literal syntax
ages := map[string]int{
    "Alice": 25,
    "Bob":   30,
}

// Empty map
var ages map[string]int // nil map (don't use!)
ages := map[string]int{} // empty map (OK)
```

### Basic Operations

```go
// Insert/Update
ages["Charlie"] = 35

// Retrieve (value, exists)
age, ok := ages["Alice"]

// Delete
delete(ages, "Bob")

// Check existence
if _, ok := ages["Alice"]; ok {
    fmt.Println("Alice exists")
}
```

### Iteration with Range

```go
for key, value := range ages {
    fmt.Println(key, value)
}
```

### Map Properties

- Keys must be comparable (no slices, maps, functions)
- No guarantee of iteration order
- Using nil map to write causes panic - always use make or literal

